course: dictionary
lesson: 2202
title: Countable and uncountable
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some nouns can be counted and some cannot. A countable noun can have a number in front of it and a plural form: «one apple, two apples». Countable is the word for this kind of noun, and it is also the word for anything that can be counted.
explain: Uncountable nouns are measured, not counted. You cannot say «two waters»; you say «a glass of water». Ask yourself: can I put a number in front of it and make it plural? If yes, it is countable.
explain: Maths uses the same idea with bigger things. A set is countable if its members can be listed one by one, first, second, third, even if the list never ends. The whole numbers are countable because you can list them in order.
fact: Countable means able to be counted.
fact: Countable nouns such as «chair», «dog» and «idea» can take «a» or «an» in the singular and «many» or «fewer» with them: «many dogs», «fewer ideas».
fact: Uncountable nouns such as «water», «rice» and «information» take «much» or «less»: «much rice», «less water».
fact: In careful writing, «fewer» goes with countable nouns and «less» goes with uncountable ones. In casual speech many people say «less» for both.
fact: Some infinite sets are countable, like the whole numbers, and some are not.
exercise: Which word is a countable noun, one you can count? | information | water | rice | *chair
exercise: Which phrase is correct for countable sweets? | much sweets | less sugar sweets | *fewer sweets | less sweets
exercise: «There are ___ apples in the bowl.» Which word fits best? | much | less | *many
test: Which word is uncountable, one you measure rather than count? | *information | apple | chair | dog
test: «There is ___ sugar in the jar.» Which word fits best? | fewer | many | *much
exam: In maths, the whole numbers 1, 2, 3 and so on can be listed one by one, even though the list never ends. What kind of set is this? | Measured | Uncountable | Empty | *Countable
